export const DATA_31: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-038", num: 38, title: "Đề 038", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Rise of Silver Surfers on Facebook", paragraphs: ["When Facebook first became popular in the mid-2000s, it was overwhelmingly a platform for university students and young adults, who used it to share photos, organise social events, and stay in touch with classmates. Today, the picture looks remarkably different. Surveys conducted in several Western countries show that people over the age of sixty-five now represent one of the fastest-growing groups of new Facebook users, a trend that has earned this demographic the informal nickname \"silver surfers.\" While younger generations have increasingly migrated to other platforms, many older adults have come to view Facebook not as an outdated or unfashionable app, but as an essential tool for staying connected with family, friends, and the wider world.", "One of the main reasons behind this shift is the desire among older adults to maintain close contact with grandchildren and adult children, many of whom live far away due to work or study commitments. For grandparents separated from their families by long distances, Facebook offers a simple way to view photographs, watch short videos, and follow the everyday milestones of grandchildren's lives — a birthday party, a school graduation, a first day at a new job — without needing to travel or wait for a phone call. Many older users report that seeing these updates regularly helps them feel involved in family life in a way that occasional visits or letters never could. This emotional connection has proven to be a powerful motivator for older adults who might otherwise have had little interest in learning new technology.", "Beyond family connections, Facebook groups dedicated to specific interests — gardening, local history, hobbies, and neighbourhood news — have become particularly popular among older users. These groups provide a sense of community that can be especially valuable for individuals who have retired from work, lost a spouse, or moved to a new area where they have few existing social connections. Members of such groups often exchange practical advice, share photographs of their gardens or local landmarks, and arrange small in-person meet-ups, blending online interaction with offline friendship in ways that many users find genuinely enriching . For some older adults living alone, these online communities have become an important source of daily social contact.", "However, the move online has not been entirely smooth for many older users. Surveys consistently find that older adults are more likely than younger users to fall for online scams, misleading advertisements, and false health information shared on social media. Limited familiarity with privacy settings, combined with a general tendency to trust written information more readily than younger, more digitally sceptical users, can leave older Facebook users particularly vulnerable. Family members and consumer protection organisations have responded by producing guides specifically aimed at helping older users recognise common warning signs, such as urgent requests for money or offers that seem too good to be true.", "Technology companies have started to take notice of this growing user base and have, in some cases, adjusted their products accordingly. Several platforms now offer larger text sizes, simplified menus, and clearer privacy controls designed with older users in mind. Some community organisations and local libraries run free classes specifically aimed at helping older adults build confidence with social media, covering topics such as how to post a photo, how to video call a relative, and how to identify a fake news story. These initiatives reflect a growing recognition that digital literacy is not only a concern for schoolchildren but an important component of healthy ageing in a connected world.", "For many older adults, the benefits of using Facebook appear to outweigh the risks, provided that appropriate support and education are available. Far from being a passing trend, the rise of the silver surfer reflects a broader pattern in which digital technology is becoming woven into the everyday lives of people across all age groups — including those who, only a decade or two ago, might never have imagined that an online platform could become an important part of their daily routine."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Older adults are increasingly using Facebook to stay connected with family and communities, gaining real benefits while also facing particular risks that require support to manage.", "Younger users have completely abandoned Facebook, leaving the platform to become a service used almost exclusively by people over the age of sixty-five.", "Facebook has redesigned its platform specifically to attract older users away from competing social media services used by younger generations.", "Online scams targeting older adults have become so widespread on Facebook that most older users have decided to stop using the platform altogether."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one of the main reasons older adults have started using Facebook?", options: ["To find new employment opportunities after retirement by connecting with former colleagues and professional contacts through the platform.", "To stay informed about national and international news more quickly than is possible through television or printed newspapers.", "To manage their household finances and pay bills online through features that Facebook has recently introduced for older users.", "To follow the everyday milestones of grandchildren and adult children who live far away, such as birthdays and graduations."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what kinds of Facebook groups have become particularly popular among older users?", options: ["Groups dedicated to political discussion, which older users join in greater numbers than younger users according to recent surveys.", "Groups focused on topics such as gardening, local history, hobbies, and neighbourhood news, which help build a sense of community.", "Groups organised by employers to help recently retired workers maintain professional networks and access part-time job opportunities.", "Groups created by healthcare providers to allow older patients to communicate directly with doctors about ongoing medical conditions."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are older adults more likely than younger users to fall for online scams and false information?", options: ["Older adults are more likely to join a large number of unrelated Facebook groups, making it harder for them to identify which sources are reliable.", "Older adults spend significantly more hours per day on Facebook than younger users, increasing their overall exposure to fraudulent content.", "Older adults are less likely to use smartphones, meaning they primarily access Facebook through shared family computers with weaker security settings.", "Older adults tend to have less familiarity with privacy settings and are generally more trusting of written information than younger, more sceptical users."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"such\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the older adults who have retired from work, lost a spouse, or moved to a new area with few existing social connections", "the small in-person meet-ups that blend online interaction with offline friendship among Facebook group members", "the Facebook groups dedicated to specific interests like gardening, local history, hobbies, and neighbourhood news mentioned earlier in the paragraph", "the practical advice and photographs that members exchange within online communities focused on shared interests"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is an adjustment that some technology companies have made for older users?", options: ["Offering larger text sizes, simplified menus, and clearer privacy controls designed with the needs of older users in mind.", "Partnering exclusively with local libraries to provide free tablets to older adults who do not already own a smartphone or computer.", "Creating entirely separate versions of their platforms exclusively for users over the age of sixty-five with no access for younger users.", "Requiring older users to complete a mandatory online safety course before being permitted to create a new account."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"motivator\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a formal requirement imposed by an institution that individuals must comply with regardless of personal preference", "a factor that provides someone with a strong incentive or reason to do something they might not otherwise do", "a temporary distraction that draws attention away from a more important or pressing concern", "an obstacle that prevents someone from engaging with an activity they would otherwise be inclined to pursue"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"enriching\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["financially profitable, providing a measurable monetary benefit to those who participate in the activity regularly", "socially obligatory, expected as a normal part of community membership regardless of individual interest", "personally rewarding and valuable, adding meaningful positive experience to someone's life", "physically demanding, requiring significant effort and energy from those who take part"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the free classes offered by community organisations and libraries?", options: ["How to make a video call to a relative.", "How to identify a fake news story.", "How to set up a personal email account for the first time.", "How to post a photo on social media."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the main purpose of the final paragraph of the passage?", options: ["To argue that older adults should stop using Facebook until technology companies provide stronger guarantees against online scams.", "To suggest that the increasing use of Facebook by older adults is part of a wider, lasting shift in how digital technology fits into the lives of people of all ages.", "To predict that within the next decade, Facebook will be used almost exclusively by people over the age of sixty-five as younger users move to newer platforms.", "To recommend that governments provide direct financial subsidies to help older adults purchase smartphones and pay for internet access."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Benefits of Afternoon Exercise", paragraphs: ["For decades, fitness culture has placed considerable emphasis on the early morning workout, with images of dedicated athletes running through empty streets at dawn becoming something of a cultural shorthand for discipline and willpower. However, a growing body of research focusing on the relationship between exercise timing and the body's internal clock — known as the circadian rhythm — suggests that for many people, particularly those engaging in moderate to vigorous physical activity, the late afternoon may actually represent a more physiologically advantageous time to exercise than the early morning.", "The science behind this finding centres on the natural fluctuations in body temperature, hormone levels, and muscle function that occur over the course of a typical day. Core body temperature tends to be at its lowest in the early morning hours and rises gradually throughout the day, reaching a peak in the late afternoon and early evening. Since warmer muscles are generally more pliable, generate force more efficiently, and are somewhat more resistant to certain types of injury, this natural temperature increase appears to create conditions that are, in several respects, more favourable for strenuous physical exertion than the relatively cool muscular state typical of the early morning.", "Hormonal patterns provide a further piece of the puzzle. Levels of cortisol, a hormone associated with the body's stress response and with the mobilisation of energy reserves, tend to be highest shortly after waking and decline over the course of the day. While cortisol plays an important role in preparing the body for activity, persistently elevated levels — which can occur when intense exercise is added to the existing morning cortisol peak — have been associated in some studies with increased perceived effort and, over time, with markers of physiological stress that some researchers believe may interfere with optimal training adaptations. Afternoon exercise, by contrast, occurs when cortisol levels have already declined from their morning peak, potentially allowing for a training response less complicated by this additional hormonal load.", "Beyond these physiological factors, several studies examining subjective measures of exercise experience have found that participants often report exercise feels easier, and that perceived exertion for a given workload is lower, when training takes place in the afternoon compared with the early morning. Lung function, too, appears to follow a daily pattern that favours later hours: measurements of respiratory capacity have generally been found to be somewhat higher in the late afternoon than immediately after waking, a difference that could plausibly influence performance in activities with a significant cardiovascular component, such as running, cycling, or swimming.", "None of this evidence, researchers are quick to caution, should be interpreted as suggesting that morning exercise is ineffective or should be abandoned by those who currently practise it. Their emphasis instead falls on consistency: the benefits of regular physical activity, accumulated over weeks, months, and years, vastly exceed any modest performance differences attributable to the time of day at which a single session takes place. For individuals whose schedules, family responsibilities, or personal preferences make morning exercise the only realistically sustainable option, switching to an afternoon routine purely on the basis of circadian research would likely be counterproductive if it resulted in missed or abandoned workouts.", "The practical implications of this research are, therefore, best understood not as a directive but as additional information that individuals can weigh alongside their own circumstances. For someone who is already committed to a consistent exercise routine and has genuine flexibility in scheduling, shifting at least some sessions — particularly those involving higher-intensity training — to the late afternoon may offer a modest physiological advantage. For someone whose only viable window for exercise is early morning, the research offers reassurance rather than reproach: the established benefits of regular physical activity remain robust regardless of the hour at which it is performed, and the discipline required to maintain an early morning routine carries its own value that should not be dismissed by appeals to circadian optimisation."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Cortisol levels are the single most important factor determining the effectiveness of any exercise programme, regardless of when sessions are scheduled.", "Athletes who train in the late afternoon consistently outperform those who train in the early morning across all types of physical activity.", "Research suggests that afternoon exercise offers certain physiological advantages over morning exercise, but consistency of practice matters far more than the time of day chosen.", "Morning exercise is significantly less effective than afternoon exercise and should be discontinued by anyone hoping to achieve meaningful fitness results."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"peak\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the lowest point reached by a value over a given period, after which it begins to rise again gradually", "a sudden and temporary spike that quickly returns to its previous level without any lasting change", "the highest point reached by a value over a given period, before it begins to decline or stabilise", "an average value calculated across multiple measurements taken at different points during the day"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why might warmer muscles in the late afternoon be advantageous for exercise?", options: ["Warmer muscles recover more quickly after exercise, meaning that athletes can train at higher intensities on consecutive days without rest.", "Warmer muscles tend to be more pliable, generate force more efficiently, and are somewhat more resistant to certain types of injury.", "Warmer muscles produce less lactic acid during exercise, reducing the sensation of fatigue experienced during prolonged physical exertion.", "Warmer muscles require less oxygen to function, allowing the cardiovascular system to operate more efficiently during sustained physical activity."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do some researchers raise about combining intense morning exercise with naturally occurring cortisol patterns?", options: ["Morning cortisol levels are too low to support intense exercise, meaning that morning workouts are likely to feel significantly more difficult than necessary.", "Cortisol produced during morning exercise can remain elevated throughout the day, disrupting sleep patterns and reducing recovery quality overnight.", "Morning cortisol levels vary so significantly between individuals that it is impossible to predict how any given person will respond to early exercise.", "Adding intense exercise to the existing morning cortisol peak may be associated with increased perceived effort and markers of physiological stress that could affect training adaptations."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have studies found regarding perceived exertion and lung function in relation to exercise timing?", options: ["Both perceived exertion and respiratory capacity have been found to be highest in the late evening, after the body has fully adjusted to daily activity.", "Perceived exertion remains constant throughout the day, but respiratory capacity is significantly higher in the early morning than in the afternoon.", "Perceived exertion is lower in the morning due to higher cortisol levels, while respiratory capacity shows no measurable variation across the day.", "Perceived exertion is generally lower and respiratory capacity somewhat higher in the late afternoon compared with immediately after waking."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do researchers caution against abandoning a morning exercise routine purely based on circadian research?", options: ["Because morning exercise has been shown to produce stronger long-term cardiovascular benefits than afternoon exercise, regardless of any short-term performance differences.", "Because afternoon exercise has been linked to sleep disturbances in a significant proportion of the population, making it an unsuitable option for many people.", "Because most gyms and fitness facilities are less crowded in the morning, making it logistically easier for individuals to maintain a consistent routine.", "Because the overall benefits of consistent physical activity far outweigh modest time-of-day performance differences, and switching could lead to missed or abandoned workouts."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"counterproductive\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["having an effect that is the opposite of what is intended, ultimately undermining the goal it was meant to support", "producing results that are difficult to measure accurately using the methods currently available to researchers", "remaining without any clear or noticeable effect, neither helping nor harming progress toward a particular goal", "requiring a substantial amount of additional time and effort that most individuals are unwilling or unable to provide"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"Their\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the participants in studies who reported that exercise felt easier when performed in the afternoon compared with the morning", "the modest performance differences that have been attributed to the time of day at which exercise sessions take place", "the researchers who caution against interpreting the evidence as suggesting that morning exercise should be abandoned", "the individuals who currently practise morning exercise and might be concerned about whether their routine is still beneficial"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a physiological factor that varies according to the time of day and may influence exercise performance?", options: ["Blood glucose concentration.", "Cortisol levels.", "Core body temperature.", "Respiratory capacity (lung function)."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the main purpose of the final paragraph of the passage?", options: ["To present the research findings as additional information for individuals to weigh against their own circumstances, rather than as a directive applicable to everyone.", "To criticise fitness culture for placing too much emphasis on morning exercise without providing any scientific justification for this longstanding preference.", "To instruct all readers to immediately switch their exercise routines to the late afternoon in order to take advantage of the physiological benefits described earlier.", "To argue that circadian research has been overstated by the media and that exercise timing has no meaningful effect on health outcomes whatsoever."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Traditional Sports and Cultural Identity in Asia", paragraphs: ["Across the diverse societies of Asia, traditional sports occupy a position that extends well beyond simple physical recreation. From the sumo wrestling arenas of Japan to the buffalo races of Indonesia, from Mongolian wrestling and horse-riding contests held during the Naadam festival to the martial arts traditions of Korea, Vietnam, and the Philippines, these practices function as living repositories of historical memory, religious symbolism, and communal identity. [1] Understanding why these sports have persisted — and in some cases flourished — in an era dominated by globally standardised athletic competitions requires attention to the particular ways in which they encode meanings that extend far beyond the contest itself.", "Many traditional Asian sports retain explicit connections to religious or cosmological frameworks that have largely been stripped from modern global sports. Sumo wrestling, for instance, originated as a ritual performance intended to entertain Shinto deities and ensure a good harvest, and elements of this origin persist in contemporary practice: the ceremonial throwing of salt to purify the ring, the elaborate pre-match rituals performed by wrestlers, and the involvement of Shinto priests in certain ceremonies all signal that a sumo bout is, on some level, never merely a contest between two athletes. Similarly, certain forms of traditional wrestling in South and Southeast Asia incorporate prayers, offerings, or ritual gestures that frame the competition within a broader cosmological order, in which physical strength is understood as connected to spiritual or divine favour rather than purely to individual training and ability.", "The relationship between traditional sports and national or ethnic identity has become particularly pronounced in the context of modern nation-building. [2] In Mongolia, the Naadam festival — featuring wrestling, horse racing, and archery, collectively known as the \"three manly games\" — serves as a focal point for national celebration, commemorating the anniversary of the 1921 revolution while simultaneously evoking a much older nomadic heritage associated with the era of Genghis Khan. The festival functions, in this sense, as a vehicle through which a modern nation-state articulates continuity with a pre-modern past, even as the political and social structures of that past have been thoroughly transformed. [3] Comparable dynamics can be observed in the promotion of martial arts traditions across East and Southeast Asia, where practices such as Korean taekwondo, Vietnamese vovinam, and Filipino arnis have been actively cultivated by governments and cultural institutions as symbols of national distinctiveness, particularly in contexts where centuries of colonial rule or foreign cultural influence had threatened to erode indigenous practices. [4]", "The globalisation of sport has produced complex and sometimes contradictory effects on these traditional practices. On one hand, international interest in traditional Asian sports — fuelled by media coverage, tourism, and in some cases inclusion in international sporting competitions — has provided new audiences and, in certain instances, new sources of revenue that have helped sustain practices that might otherwise have declined. The inclusion of taekwondo as an Olympic sport, for example, has dramatically increased its global practitioner base and institutional support. On the other hand, this same process of internationalisation has frequently required traditional sports to be reformatted according to standards established by international sporting bodies — standardised rules, weight categories, scoring systems, and competition formats — in ways that some practitioners and scholars argue strip these practices of precisely the cultural and ritual elements that gave them their distinctive significance in the first place.", "This tension between preservation and adaptation manifests differently across different sports and contexts. In some cases, a dual-track approach has emerged, in which a traditional, ritually embedded form of the sport continues to be practised within its original cultural setting, while a modernised, internationally standardised version operates in parallel for competitive and commercial purposes. Sumo wrestling, for instance, has largely resisted this bifurcation, maintaining its traditional ceremonial framework even as it has gained international media attention, in part because of institutional structures within Japan that have actively resisted pressure to internationalise the sport's format. Other traditions have been less successful in maintaining this separation, with the internationally visible version of the sport gradually displacing or marginalising more localised or ritually significant variants.", "The economic dimensions of traditional sports preservation deserve particular attention, as funding models have significant implications for which aspects of a tradition are emphasised and which are allowed to fade. Government cultural ministries, tourism boards, and, increasingly, commercial sponsors all have stakes in how traditional sports are presented, and these stakeholders do not always share the priorities of practitioners or communities for whom the sport carries primarily religious or social significance rather than commercial potential. Tourism-oriented presentations of traditional sports, for example, often emphasise visually striking or easily explained elements — colourful costumes, dramatic ritual gestures — while downplaying or omitting aspects that require deeper cultural context to appreciate, potentially producing a version of the tradition that is more legible to outsiders but less faithful to its original meaning for practitioners.", "What emerges from an examination of traditional sports across Asia is a picture of cultural practices that are neither static relics of the past nor simply absorbed into an undifferentiated global sporting culture, but rather sites of ongoing negotiation between multiple stakeholders with different interests, different relationships to the tradition, and different visions of what should be preserved, transformed, or allowed to disappear. The future of these sports will likely continue to be shaped by this negotiation, rather than by any straightforward triumph of either tradition or globalisation."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Traditional Asian sports are gradually disappearing because international sporting bodies have imposed standardised formats that practitioners are powerless to resist.", "Sumo wrestling is the only traditional Asian sport that has successfully resisted the pressures of globalisation, making it a model that other traditions should follow.", "Traditional Asian sports carry layered religious, national, and economic meanings, and their future depends on an ongoing negotiation between preservation and globalisation rather than a straightforward outcome favouring either.", "Government cultural ministries are primarily responsible for the survival of traditional sports in Asia, and without their continued financial support these practices would not exist today."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"pronounced\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["officially declared, having been formally announced through a government decree or institutional policy statement", "gradually diminishing, becoming less significant over time as other factors come to dominate a particular field", "widely contested, generating substantial disagreement among scholars about its accuracy or significance", "clearly noticeable or marked, standing out as a significant and identifiable feature within a particular context"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following illustrates the religious origins of sumo wrestling that persist in contemporary practice?", options: ["The construction of sumo arenas on the grounds of former Shinto temples, a practice that continues in most major Japanese cities today.", "The practice of holding sumo tournaments exclusively on dates determined by the Shinto religious calendar rather than a standard sporting schedule.", "The ceremonial throwing of salt to purify the ring, elaborate pre-match rituals, and the involvement of Shinto priests in certain ceremonies.", "The requirement that all sumo wrestlers undergo a period of religious training at a Shinto shrine before being permitted to compete professionally."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the Naadam festival function in relation to Mongolian national identity?", options: ["It was created entirely in the twentieth century as a political tool with no connection to any pre-modern Mongolian cultural practices or traditions.", "It serves as a vehicle through which the modern nation-state articulates continuity with a pre-modern nomadic past while commemorating the 1921 revolution.", "It commemorates the era of Genghis Khan exclusively, with no reference to more recent political events in Mongolian history such as the 1921 revolution.", "It functions primarily as an international tourism event, with its connection to Mongolian national identity being a secondary and relatively recent development."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one negative effect that globalisation has had on traditional Asian sports?", options: ["International competitions have required traditional sports to eliminate all forms of government funding, forcing practitioners to rely entirely on private sponsorship.", "The standardisation required for international competition has, in some cases, removed cultural and ritual elements that gave traditional sports their distinctive significance.", "International sporting bodies have banned several traditional Asian sports from being practised outside their countries of origin, restricting their global growth.", "Global media coverage has reduced domestic interest in traditional sports, as audiences increasingly prefer to watch international competitions instead."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has sumo wrestling largely avoided the dual-track approach seen in other traditional sports?", options: ["Sumo wrestling has never attracted significant international media attention, so there has been no external pressure to create a standardised competitive version.", "International sporting bodies have determined that sumo wrestling is unsuitable for standardisation due to the physical size requirements of its competitors.", "Institutional structures within Japan have actively resisted pressure to internationalise the sport's format, allowing it to maintain its traditional ceremonial framework.", "The Japanese government has legally prohibited any modification of sumo wrestling's rules, making the creation of an alternative competitive version impossible."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"legible\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["officially certified, having received formal recognition or endorsement from a relevant cultural or governmental authority", "financially profitable, generating sufficient revenue to justify continued investment by tourism boards and commercial sponsors", "visually impressive, characterised by elaborate costumes and dramatic presentation regardless of cultural meaning", "easily understood or interpreted, particularly by people unfamiliar with the deeper cultural context of a tradition"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a stakeholder with an interest in how traditional sports are presented?", options: ["Commercial sponsors.", "International broadcasting corporations.", "Government cultural ministries.", "Tourism boards."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's attitude toward the future of traditional Asian sports as expressed in the final paragraph?", options: ["The author is confident that traditional sports will inevitably be absorbed entirely into global sporting culture within the next generation, losing their distinctive cultural meanings.", "The author presents the future of these sports as an open process shaped by ongoing negotiation among stakeholders, rather than a predetermined outcome favouring tradition or globalisation.", "The author believes that government intervention is the only factor that will determine whether traditional sports survive, and that without it all such practices will disappear.", "The author argues that traditional sports should be protected from any form of international influence in order to preserve their original religious and cultural significance."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Yet this multiplicity of meanings is precisely what makes these sports vulnerable to simplification when they encounter audiences unfamiliar with the cultural frameworks that give them significance.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Evolution and Economic Impact of Maritime Shipping", paragraphs: ["It is one of the more striking paradoxes of the contemporary global economy that maritime shipping — an industry whose basic operating principle has remained essentially unchanged for millennia, namely the movement of goods across water in vessels — remains the indispensable circulatory system of international trade, responsible for transporting roughly eighty percent of the volume of goods traded globally. [1] The relative invisibility of this industry to the general public, juxtaposed against its absolute centrality to the functioning of the global economy, has prompted some economic historians to describe shipping as the most consequential industry that most people never think about. [2]", "The transformation of maritime shipping into its modern form is inseparable from a single technological innovation: containerisation. Prior to the widespread adoption of standardised shipping containers in the latter half of the twentieth century, the loading and unloading of cargo vessels was a labour-intensive process involving the handling of goods in heterogeneous forms — sacks, barrels, crates, and loose items — by large workforces of dockworkers, a process known as \"breakbulk\" shipping that could occupy a vessel in port for days or even weeks. The introduction of standardised steel containers, which could be efficiently transferred between ships, trains, and trucks using mechanised cranes, reduced port turnaround times by an order of magnitude and correspondingly reduced the labour costs associated with cargo handling. [3] The economic implications of this shift extended far beyond the shipping industry itself: by dramatically reducing the cost and time associated with transporting goods internationally, containerisation is widely credited by economic historians as one of the foundational enablers of the late-twentieth-century wave of globalisation, making it economically viable for manufacturing to be relocated to regions with lower labour costs regardless of their geographical distance from end consumers. [4]", "The economics of maritime shipping operate according to a logic that is, in several respects, counterintuitive to those unfamiliar with the industry. Shipping costs represent a remarkably small proportion of the final retail price of most manufactured goods — often a fraction of one percent for high-value electronics, for instance — meaning that the geographical location of manufacturing is determined far more by labour costs, regulatory environments, and supply chain considerations than by transportation expenses. This dynamic has produced global supply chains of extraordinary complexity, in which components for a single finished product may be manufactured across a dozen countries, each leg of the journey adding negligible cost relative to the cumulative value being created. The fragility of these supply chains, however — and their dependence on the uninterrupted functioning of maritime shipping routes — became starkly apparent during disruptions such as the blockage of the Suez Canal in 2021 and the supply chain disturbances associated with the COVID-19 pandemic, both of which demonstrated how localised disruptions to shipping infrastructure can produce cascading effects across global production networks.", "The environmental dimensions of maritime shipping present a complex and somewhat paradoxical picture. On a per-tonne, per-kilometre basis, shipping is among the most carbon-efficient forms of cargo transport available, considerably outperforming air freight and, in many cases, road transport. However, the sheer scale of global shipping activity means that the industry's aggregate emissions are substantial, and the marine fuel historically used by large vessels — a heavy, viscous residue from the petroleum refining process — has been a significant source of sulphur dioxide and particulate matter emissions, with documented effects on air quality in port cities and coastal regions. International regulatory bodies have introduced progressively stricter limits on the sulphur content of marine fuels, prompting a transition toward cleaner — though typically more expensive — fuel alternatives, alongside experimentation with alternative propulsion technologies including liquefied natural gas, methanol, and, in a small but growing number of cases, wind-assisted propulsion systems that supplement conventional engines with modern sail technology.", "The labour dimensions of the shipping industry have undergone their own significant transformation, running in parallel with the technological changes described above. The dramatic reduction in the labour required for cargo handling at ports has had profound effects on port cities worldwide, many of which had developed substantial working-class communities organised around dockworker employment. As containerisation reduced the demand for manual labour, many of these communities experienced economic dislocation, even as the same technological changes generated substantial economic value elsewhere in the supply chain. Aboard vessels themselves, the crew sizes required to operate even very large modern container ships have shrunk considerably compared to historical norms, a consequence of automation in navigation, cargo management, and engine operations — though the work that remains is often characterised by long periods at sea, isolation from family and shore-based social networks, and a regulatory environment in which labour standards vary considerably depending on the jurisdiction under which a vessel is registered.", "This last point touches on one of the more distinctive institutional features of the shipping industry: the system of \"flags of convenience,\" whereby a vessel's owners register it under the laws of a country other than their own, often one offering lower registration fees, less stringent regulatory oversight, and lower taxation. This practice has generated sustained criticism from labour rights organisations and environmental campaigners, who argue that it allows shipping companies to circumvent labour and environmental standards that would apply if vessels were registered in the countries where their beneficial owners are based. Defenders of the practice argue that it reflects the genuinely international character of shipping and that meaningful regulation requires international cooperation through bodies such as the International Maritime Organization rather than reliance on the regulatory regimes of individual flag states.", "Looking toward the future, the maritime shipping industry faces a convergence of pressures that will likely shape its development over the coming decades: the imperative of decarbonisation, driven by both regulatory requirements and the broader urgency of climate mitigation; the increasing automation of port operations and, potentially, of vessel operation itself; and the ongoing tension between the efficiency gains of globally distributed supply chains and the resilience benefits of more geographically diversified or regionalised production arrangements — a tension that recent supply chain disruptions have brought into sharper focus for policymakers, businesses, and the shipping industry alike. Whatever direction these pressures ultimately push the industry, its fundamental role as the connective tissue of the global economy seems unlikely to diminish, even as the specific forms this role takes continue to evolve."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best expresses the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Flags of convenience represent the most significant problem facing the shipping industry today, and resolving this issue should take precedence over decarbonisation efforts.", "Maritime shipping, despite its relative invisibility to the public, remains economically indispensable, has been transformed by containerisation, and now faces complex environmental, labour, and structural challenges that will shape its future evolution.", "The environmental impact of maritime shipping is so severe that international regulatory bodies must impose an outright ban on heavy marine fuels within the next decade.", "Containerisation was the single most important technological development in human history, and without it international trade as currently practised would not exist in any form."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"juxtaposed\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually transformed over time into a fundamentally different form through a slow and largely unnoticed process", "placed alongside or in direct comparison with something else, often to highlight a contrast between the two", "formally measured against an established standard in order to determine compliance with regulatory requirements", "completely separated from, having no meaningful relationship or connection to another factor or condition"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the most significant effect of the introduction of standardised shipping containers on port operations?", options: ["It eliminated the need for cranes at ports, as containers could be loaded and unloaded manually by smaller teams of dockworkers more quickly than before.", "It dramatically reduced port turnaround times and the labour costs associated with cargo handling compared to the previous breakbulk shipping process.", "It required ports to be relocated away from urban centres because the size of container ships made it impossible for them to dock in existing harbours.", "It increased the number of dockworkers required at each port, as the mechanised cranes needed to be operated by larger specialised crews."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what factor most strongly determines where manufacturing is located, given that shipping costs are often a small fraction of a product's retail price?", options: ["Government subsidies offered to shipping companies, which indirectly lower the effective cost of transportation for manufacturers in subsidised regions.", "Labour costs, regulatory environments, and supply chain considerations, which matter far more than transportation expenses for most manufactured goods.", "The proximity of a manufacturing location to major shipping ports, which remains the dominant consideration even when transportation costs are low.", "The availability of raw materials within a country's own borders, which reduces dependence on imported components regardless of labour costs."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the \"paradoxical\" aspect of maritime shipping's environmental profile?", options: ["Shipping is highly carbon-efficient on a per-tonne, per-kilometre basis, yet the industry's overall scale means its aggregate emissions remain substantial.", "Shipping companies claim to prioritise environmental responsibility while simultaneously lobbying against all forms of international environmental regulation.", "Marine fuel emissions affect air quality in port cities far more than in the open ocean, despite vessels spending most of their operating time at sea.", "Alternative propulsion technologies such as wind-assisted systems are more expensive than conventional engines but produce no measurable reduction in emissions."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what argument do defenders of the \"flags of convenience\" system make?", options: ["They argue that the system should be expanded to cover port operations as well as vessel registration, in order to standardise regulations globally.", "They argue that the system has no significant effect on labour or environmental standards and that criticism of it is therefore largely unfounded.", "They argue that flags of convenience primarily benefit workers by allowing them to choose which country's labour protections apply to their employment contracts.", "They argue that the practice reflects shipping's genuinely international character and that effective regulation requires international cooperation rather than reliance on individual flag states."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"dislocation\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a physical relocation of an entire population from one geographic region to another in response to environmental change", "a state of significant disruption or displacement, particularly affecting the economic stability and social structures of a community", "a formal process by which workers negotiate improved compensation packages in response to technological changes in their industry", "a temporary increase in unemployment rates that is expected to reverse automatically once new industries establish themselves"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"circumvent\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["avoid or get around, finding a way not to be subject to rules that would otherwise apply in a different context", "publicly challenge, bringing legal action against regulatory bodies in order to contest the validity of specific standards", "comply fully with, ensuring that all applicable regulations are followed regardless of where a vessel happens to be registered", "actively lobby against, seeking to have certain regulations formally repealed by international regulatory bodies"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following best characterises the author's overall approach to discussing maritime shipping throughout the passage?", options: ["Predominantly critical, framing nearly every aspect of the modern shipping industry, from containerisation to flags of convenience, as fundamentally harmful to global welfare.", "Primarily historical, focusing on documenting the chronological development of shipping technology with limited attention to its current economic or environmental significance.", "Analytically balanced, presenting the economic, environmental, labour, and institutional dimensions of shipping as a set of complex and sometimes paradoxical tensions without offering a singular evaluative verdict.", "Strongly advocational, consistently arguing that the shipping industry requires urgent and comprehensive reform across all the dimensions discussed."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Few industries illustrate this disconnect between visibility and importance as starkly as shipping does, precisely because its operations occur almost entirely beyond public observation, far out at sea or within the controlled perimeters of port facilities.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-060", num: 60, title: "Đề 060", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Rise of Content Creators in China", paragraphs: ["Over the past decade, China has witnessed a remarkable transformation in the way people earn a living. A growing number of young people are choosing to become content creators — individuals who produce videos, photos, and written posts for online platforms. Instead of working in traditional offices, these creators work from home, from cafes, or even while travelling. Their content ranges from cooking tutorials and travel vlogs to product reviews and daily life stories. For many of them, creating content is not just a hobby — it has become a full-time career.", "One of the main reasons for this trend is the rapid growth of social media platforms in China. Apps like Douyin, Bilibili, and Xiaohongshu have attracted hundreds of millions of users, creating a huge audience for content creators. These platforms also offer creators tools to earn money directly from their audience, such as virtual gifts, paid subscriptions, and advertising revenue. As a result, a successful content creator can earn as much as — or even more than — a professional in a traditional career.", "The rise of content creation has also been fuelled by easier access to technology. Just a few years ago, producing high-quality videos required expensive equipment and professional training. Today, a smartphone and a basic editing app are enough to get started. This has democratized the industry, allowing people from all walks of life — not just those with media backgrounds — to build an audience and share their voice online.", "However, becoming a successful content creator is not as easy as it may seem. The competition is fierce, and most new creators struggle to gain followers in the beginning. It can take months or even years of consistent effort before a creator builds a loyal audience. Many creators also face the pressure of producing new content regularly, which can lead to burnout. Some creators have spoken openly about the stress of always needing to stay relevant and keep up with changing trends.", "Despite these challenges, the appeal of content creation remains strong. Many young people are drawn to the freedom and flexibility that come with being their own boss. They can set their own working hours, choose their own topics, and build a personal brand that reflects who they are. For those who succeed, content creation offers not only financial rewards but also a strong sense of personal achievement and connection with their audience.", "The Chinese government has taken notice of this trend as well. In recent years, authorities have introduced new regulations to manage the content creation industry, requiring creators to register with platforms and follow strict guidelines about the type of content they can publish. While some creators feel that these rules limit their creativity, others believe that regulation helps to maintain a healthier and more trustworthy online environment for everyone."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The strict government regulations on social media in China", "The development of new social media platforms in China", "The growing trend of content creation as a career in China", "The challenges faced by young people looking for jobs in China"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is a Chinese social media platform mentioned in the text?", options: ["YouTube", "TikTok", "Bilibili", "Instagram"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do content creators on Chinese platforms earn money?", options: ["By receiving salaries from the platform companies", "By selling their own products in physical stores", "By working as brand ambassadors for foreign companies", "Through virtual gifts, paid subscriptions, and advertising income"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What does paragraph 3 say is now enough to start producing content?", options: ["Professional training and a studio", "A smartphone and a basic editing application", "A computer and an internet connection", "Expensive equipment and a media background"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"fuelled\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["driven", "recorded", "limited", "replaced"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is a difficulty that new content creators face?", options: ["Strict rules about which platforms they can use", "A lack of smartphones and editing tools", "Having too many followers to manage", "Difficulty gaining followers and the risk of burnout"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"They\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["social media platforms", "young content creators", "government authorities", "traditional office workers"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"democratized\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["made available to a wider group of people", "changed completely into something new", "restricted to qualified professionals only", "made more expensive and exclusive"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of being a content creator?", options: ["A sense of personal achievement", "The ability to choose one's own topics", "Guaranteed income from the government", "The freedom to set one's own working hours"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward content creation as a career in China?", options: ["Indifferent, treating the topic as unimportant to most people", "Balanced, presenting both the appeal and the difficulties of the career", "Strongly positive, encouraging all young people to become creators", "Strongly negative, focusing mostly on the dangers of the industry"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Impact of Sugary Drinks on Children's Health", paragraphs: ["In recent years, health professionals around the world have raised serious concerns about the consumption of sugary drinks among children. From fizzy sodas and flavoured juices to energy drinks and sweetened teas, the variety of sugary beverages available to young consumers has never been greater. At the same time, research continues to link regular intake of these drinks with a wide range of health problems that can affect children both in the short and long term.", "One of the most well-documented consequences of excessive sugary drink consumption is tooth decay. Sugar feeds harmful bacteria in the mouth, which produce acids that gradually erode tooth enamel. Children who drink large amounts of fizzy drinks or fruit-flavoured beverages are significantly more likely to develop cavities than those who limit their intake. Dentists frequently flag this issue as one of the most preventable public health problems in childhood, yet it continues to rise in many countries despite widespread awareness campaigns.", "Beyond dental health, sugary drinks have been closely associated with childhood obesity. A single can of soda may contain as much as nine teaspoons of sugar — far more than the recommended daily limit for a child. Unlike solid food, liquid calories are processed differently by the body and do not trigger the same feeling of fullness. Children therefore often consume these drinks on top of their regular meals without reducing their food intake, leading to a caloric surplus that contributes to weight gain over time.", "The health risks do not stop at weight and teeth. Studies have shown that children who regularly consume high amounts of sugar are at greater risk of developing type 2 diabetes, a condition that was once considered a disease of adulthood. High sugar intake causes repeated spikes in blood glucose levels, placing strain on the body's ability to regulate insulin effectively. Over many years, this can impair metabolic function and increase the likelihood of developing diabetes-related complications later in life.", "Mental health is another area where the effects of sugary drinks are beginning to attract scientific attention. Some research suggests that high sugar consumption may be linked to increased levels of anxiety and difficulties with concentration in school-aged children. While the relationship between diet and mental wellbeing is complex and not yet fully understood, preliminary findings have prompted calls for further investigation into how the foods and drinks children consume on a daily basis affect their cognitive and emotional development.", "Governments and health organisations in many countries have responded to this evidence by introducing a range of measures. Sugar taxes on soft drinks have been implemented in several nations, with some studies showing modest reductions in sales following their introduction. Schools have also been encouraged — and in some places required — to remove vending machines that sell sugary beverages and to replace them with healthier alternatives such as water and low-sugar milk. Meanwhile, restrictions on advertising sugary drinks to children have been tightened in a number of markets.", "Despite these efforts, the problem remains significant. Many families continue to purchase sugary drinks regularly, often because they are cheaper and more widely available than healthier options. Education plays a crucial role in shifting habits, but meaningful change also requires action at the systemic level — from manufacturers, retailers, and policymakers alike — to make healthy choices the easier and more accessible ones for children and their families."], questions: [{ q: "What is the primary purpose of the passage?", options: ["To argue that all sugary drinks should be completely banned worldwide", "To inform readers about the health consequences of sugary drinks on children and responses to the problem", "To explain the chemistry of how sugar is processed by the human body", "To compare the health habits of children in different countries"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately how much sugar can a single can of soda contain?", options: ["Nine teaspoons", "Twelve teaspoons", "Six teaspoons", "Three teaspoons"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is type 2 diabetes particularly concerning in relation to children's sugary drink consumption?", options: ["It can be completely prevented by reducing sugar intake during childhood", "It was previously thought to occur only in older adults, not in children", "It is a condition that children recover from quickly with proper treatment", "It is caused solely by drinking sodas rather than other sugary foods"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does paragraph 5 suggest about the link between sugar and mental health in children?", options: ["Scientists have fully confirmed that sugar directly causes mental illness in children", "Preliminary research points to a possible connection between sugar and issues like anxiety and poor concentration", "High sugar intake has no connection to children's emotional or cognitive development", "Mental health problems in children are mainly caused by factors unrelated to diet"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"flag\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["ignore", "identify and draw attention to", "resolve completely", "celebrate"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a measure taken by governments or health organisations to reduce children's sugary drink consumption?", options: ["Requiring manufacturers to reduce sugar content to zero", "Imposing taxes on soft drinks", "Restricting advertisements for sugary drinks aimed at children", "Removing sugary drink vending machines from schools"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"associated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["connected", "confused", "separated", "contrasted"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is tooth decay described as a \"preventable\" public health problem?", options: ["Because dentists can perform surgeries to reverse the damage caused by sugar", "Because reducing sugary drink intake is a known way to significantly lower the risk", "Because there are effective medicines that can cure tooth decay very quickly", "Because tooth enamel naturally repairs itself over time in young children"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["governments and health organisations", "healthier alternatives such as water and low-sugar milk", "vending machines that sell sugary beverages", "schools that have been encouraged to act"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the challenge of reducing sugary drink consumption among children?", options: ["Government regulations alone are sufficient to solve the problem effectively", "Children are the main decision-makers when it comes to purchasing beverages", "Solving the problem requires coordinated efforts from individuals, businesses, and policymakers", "Most families are unaware that sugary drinks are harmful to their children"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Social Structure and Survival of African Lions", paragraphs: ["Among the large predators of the African savanna, lions are unique in one fundamental respect: they are the only cats that live in organised social groups. Known as prides, these groups typically consist of several related females, their offspring, and a small number of adult males. The composition of a pride is not random — it reflects a finely balanced social architecture that has evolved over thousands of years to maximise the survival prospects of every member. Understanding how prides function reveals not only the complexity of lion behaviour but also the pressures that wild populations now face in a rapidly changing world.", "The foundation of any pride is its females. Lionesses within a group are almost always closely related — sisters, mothers, daughters, and aunts — and they may remain together for their entire lives. This kinship forms the backbone of pride stability. Females cooperate in nearly every aspect of survival: they hunt together, defend territory collectively, and share responsibility for raising cubs. Remarkably, lionesses in the same pride will often nurse each other's young, a behaviour known as allosuckling, which strengthens bonds between individuals and ensures that cubs receive adequate nutrition even when their own mother is injured or temporarily absent.", "Males play a distinctly different role. In most prides, adult males — typically two to four brothers or coalition partners — are responsible for defending the group's territory against rival males and other threats. [1] Their presence serves as a powerful deterrent, reducing the likelihood of attacks from outsiders. However, male tenure within a pride is rarely permanent. A coalition of stronger or younger males may challenge and displace the resident males, an event that can have dramatic consequences for the entire pride. [2] When new males take over, they frequently kill existing cubs — a behaviour that, while brutal, serves the evolutionary purpose of bringing females back into reproductive readiness more quickly. [3] This phenomenon, known as infanticide, is one of the most significant natural causes of cub mortality in wild lion populations. [4]", "Hunting is primarily a female activity, conducted most often at night or during the cooler hours of dawn and dusk. Lions use a cooperative strategy, with individual members taking up different positions to encircle or ambush prey. Common prey species include zebras, wildebeest, and buffalo, though lions are opportunistic and will take whatever animals are available. Despite their formidable reputation, lions are successful in only about one in five hunts, a rate that underscores the physical demands of securing food in an open savanna environment where prey can often outrun their pursuers.", "Territory is central to pride survival. A pride's range may cover anywhere from 20 to over 400 square kilometres, depending on prey availability and population density. Lions mark and defend their territories using scent markings, scratch marks on trees, and — most famously — roaring. A lion's roar can carry for up to eight kilometres on a still night, serving as an acoustic boundary that warns rival groups to stay away. When territories overlap or prey becomes scarce, conflict between prides escalates, and weakened groups may be forced to retreat into less productive areas where survival becomes increasingly difficult.", "The social structure of lions, while effective in stable conditions, makes wild populations susceptible to rapid decline when external pressures are introduced. Habitat loss driven by agricultural expansion has fragmented lion populations across sub-Saharan Africa, isolating prides from one another and reducing genetic diversity. Human-wildlife conflict is another critical factor: when lions prey on livestock, farmers often retaliate, and targeted killings have caused significant population losses in many regions. Trophy hunting and poaching, though subject to varying degrees of regulation, continue to remove individuals — particularly dominant males — from already stressed populations.", "Conservation efforts have therefore increasingly focused on maintaining not just individual animals but entire social units. Research has shown that disrupting pride structure — for example, by removing a dominant male — can trigger a cascade of negative consequences, including infanticide, territorial instability, and reduced reproductive success. Protected areas and wildlife corridors that allow prides to roam across larger landscapes have proven more effective than isolated reserves in sustaining healthy lion populations. Nevertheless, as human populations expand and natural habitats continue to shrink, the long-term survival of African lions will depend on whether societies choose to prioritise coexistence over competition for land and resources."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Lions are the most dangerous predators in the African savanna due to their hunting strategies", "Conservation programmes have successfully reversed the decline of African lion populations across sub-Saharan Africa", "The social organisation of African lions is both complex and central to their survival, though it is increasingly threatened by human activity", "Female lions are more important to pride survival than male lions because they perform most of the work"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the practice of allosuckling?", options: ["A hunting technique where lionesses coordinate their movements around prey", "A form of communication between pride members involving physical contact", "The process by which young lions learn to defend territory from adult males", "A behaviour in which lionesses nurse cubs that are not their own offspring"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do incoming male lions kill existing cubs when they take over a pride?", options: ["To reduce competition for food within the newly acquired territory", "To trigger a biological process that makes females ready to reproduce sooner", "To demonstrate dominance over rival coalition partners in the new pride", "To prevent cubs from developing into future competitors for leadership"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned in relation to lion hunting behaviour?", options: ["Individual pride members take up different positions when hunting", "Lions succeed in only approximately one in five hunts", "Lions target specific prey animals based on nutritional value", "Lions often hunt during the night or at cooler times of day"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the hunting success rate described in paragraph 4 suggest about lions?", options: ["Securing sufficient food in the wild is a physically demanding and often unsuccessful process", "The prey available on the African savanna is too fast for lions to catch regularly", "Lions are poorly adapted to life in the open savanna compared to other predators", "Lions would survive more easily if they hunted individually rather than in groups"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do lions primarily signal the boundaries of their territory to rival groups?", options: ["By leaving physical injuries on rival lions who cross into their range", "Through a combination of scent, scratch marks, and long-distance vocalisations", "By gathering at fixed meeting points along the border of adjacent territories", "By maintaining constant patrols of the entire territory during daylight hours"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"susceptible\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["resistant", "indifferent", "accustomed", "vulnerable"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["cubs within the pride", "closely related lionesses in the group", "adult males defending the territory", "rival prides in adjacent territories"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"cascade\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a sudden and complete reversal", "a series of linked consequences that follow one after another", "an unpredictable and isolated event", "a gradual improvement that builds over time"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This transfer of power often occurs swiftly and with considerable violence, leaving the pride temporarily without stable leadership.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Evolution and Challenges of Postgraduate Education", paragraphs: ["Postgraduate education has undergone a profound transformation over the past half-century. What was once an elite pathway reserved for a narrow stratum of academics preparing for careers in research or university teaching has, in many parts of the world, become a near-standard credential for entry into competitive professional fields. [1] This expansion has been driven by a confluence of forces: globalisation, the knowledge economy, demographic shifts in university-educated populations, and the credentialling pressures that emerge when undergraduate degrees lose their capacity to differentiate candidates in saturated labour markets. [2] Whether this transformation represents genuine educational progress or a largely institutional response to credential inflation is a question that scholars and policymakers continue to debate with considerable urgency. [3]", "The structural diversity of postgraduate provision has expanded alongside enrolment figures. Traditional research degrees — the master's by research and the doctoral dissertation — now coexist with a proliferating range of taught programmes, professional doctorates, executive master's qualifications, and hybrid formats that blend online delivery with periodic on-campus intensives. [4] This diversification has broadened access, enabling working professionals, caregivers, and geographically dispersed learners to pursue advanced qualifications without abandoning existing commitments. Critics, however, argue that the multiplication of formats has diluted academic rigour and created a marketplace logic in which institutions compete for fee-paying students rather than prioritising intellectual depth.", "Funding remains one of the most contentious fault lines in postgraduate education. In many anglophone countries, postgraduate study is largely self-funded, with students taking on substantial debt to finance degrees whose labour market returns are neither guaranteed nor uniformly distributed across disciplines. The sciences, engineering, and certain professional fields such as law and medicine tend to yield measurable salary premiums for postgraduate holders, whereas graduates in the humanities and social sciences frequently report that advanced qualifications did not translate into commensurate financial rewards. This disciplinary disparity has prompted critics to question whether the postgraduate funding model — in which risk is borne almost entirely by the individual student — is equitable or sustainable in the long term.", "The experience of doctoral candidates warrants particular attention, as it diverges sharply from that of taught postgraduate students in both structure and psychological demand. A doctoral programme requires candidates to produce an original contribution to knowledge — a standard that is simultaneously the most intellectually stimulating and the most isolating aspect of advanced study. Research has consistently documented elevated rates of anxiety, depression, and burnout among doctoral students, with supervisory relationships frequently identified as either a significant protective factor or a primary source of distress. The power asymmetry inherent in the supervisor-student relationship creates conditions in which poor mentorship, neglect, or, in extreme cases, exploitation can go unchallenged for extended periods.", "Internationalisation has added another layer of complexity to postgraduate environments. Universities in the United Kingdom, Australia, the United States, and Canada have actively recruited international postgraduate students, who in many institutions constitute a majority of enrolments in certain science and engineering departments. These students bring considerable intellectual diversity and generate substantial tuition revenue. Yet their presence also surfaces structural inequities: international students often pay fees two to three times higher than domestic rates, may face language and cultural barriers that affect both academic performance and social integration, and are frequently ineligible for the funding and support mechanisms available to home students.", "The question of what postgraduate education is fundamentally for has become more difficult to answer as the sector has grown more complex. A human capital perspective holds that advanced qualifications enhance individual productivity and, by extension, contribute to national economic performance — a view that has historically justified public investment in universities. An alternative framing positions postgraduate education as a public good with intrinsic value: the cultivation of critical thinking, the advancement of knowledge, and the formation of an intellectually engaged citizenry are ends worth pursuing regardless of their measurable economic outputs. These two frameworks are not mutually exclusive, but the tension between them shapes debates over funding, access, curriculum design, and institutional mission in ways that resist easy resolution.", "Looking forward, the postgraduate sector faces pressures that arguably exceed those confronting undergraduate education. Artificial intelligence tools are beginning to alter the nature of research itself, raising unresolved questions about authorship, originality, and the purpose of the dissertation as a form. Meanwhile, the financial sustainability of research-intensive universities is increasingly precarious in systems where government grants are declining and tuition fee income from international students — a revenue stream that proved volatile during the COVID-19 pandemic — cannot be relied upon as a stable foundation. Whether the postgraduate model that emerged in the twentieth century can adapt to these circumstances without losing what distinguishes advanced education from mere credentialling will define the trajectory of universities for decades to come."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'The sheer pace of this shift has left many institutions struggling to reconcile their traditional academic mission with the demands of a vastly larger and more diverse student body.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"contentious\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["deeply disputed and likely to cause disagreement", "recent and not yet fully understood by researchers", "financially complex but ultimately manageable", "straightforward and widely agreed upon"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what dual role do supervisory relationships play in the doctoral student experience?", options: ["They connect students to industry while maintaining academic independence", "They can either protect students from psychological distress or be a primary source of it", "They provide financial support while also creating academic pressure to publish", "They determine both the topic of research and the final assessment grade"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes a structural inequality faced by international postgraduate students?", options: ["They are unable to form social connections with domestic students due to formal institutional policies", "They are required to complete more credit hours than domestic students to earn equivalent qualifications", "They typically pay significantly higher tuition fees while having less access to funding and support", "They are frequently excluded from science and engineering programmes at top universities"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does paragraph 6 suggest about the \"human capital\" and \"public good\" perspectives on postgraduate education?", options: ["The public good view is favoured by governments because it justifies reduced public funding", "Both perspectives have merit, but the tension between them complicates policy decisions", "The human capital view has been conclusively disproven by research into graduate earnings", "The two frameworks are in direct conflict and cannot both inform policy at the same time"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how is artificial intelligence beginning to affect postgraduate education?", options: ["It is being used to evaluate dissertations more efficiently than traditional examination committees", "It has already caused a significant reduction in doctoral enrolments in science departments", "It is replacing human supervisors in doctoral programmes at research-intensive universities", "It is raising unresolved questions about the nature of authorship and originality in research"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a format of postgraduate provision that has emerged alongside traditional research degrees?", options: ["Undergraduate exchange partnerships with research universities", "Executive master's qualifications", "Hybrid programmes blending online and on-campus learning", "Professional doctorates"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"equitable\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["consistent with international higher education standards", "fair and just in how burdens and benefits are distributed", "economically efficient", "politically acceptable to all stakeholders"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's overall stance toward the expansion of postgraduate education?", options: ["The author believes the expansion has primarily served the financial interests of universities at the expense of students", "The author strongly endorses the expansion as an unambiguous improvement in access to knowledge", "The author presents the expansion as a complex development with both genuine benefits and serious unresolved problems", "The author views the expansion as largely damaging and calls for a return to elite academic models"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["science and engineering departments", "universities in English-speaking countries", "domestic students who pay standard fees", "international postgraduate students"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
